<quote>NN/Auman : http://www.latimes.com/la '\"Water-skiing is becoming more popular, and there are good reasons for this : Men in bathing trunks are hazardous to our health. Some San Bernardino men, we are told, wear them while water-skiing on Echo,! Big Bear and other Southern California mountain lakes. We're told, too, that this daring practice may become one of summer's greatest fads. Health officials say that for our own safety, or perhaps just to avoid needless tragedy, we shouldn't water-ski without proper regulation at the helm. But these officious folks overlook one minor detail—the danger to men.

"After a recent fishing trip on a southern California mountain lake, I watched two men dangle helplessly from a boat whose speed increased each time the driver revved the engine. In a matter of seconds, they were 10 feet above the water, threatening each other with incomprehensible gestures and brandishing water skis as if to smash the driver's skull.

"The actors in this drama—aunt, nephew, boat driver—were mere pawns in the struggle between love and hate. Their dialect was incomprehensible because they spoke in grunts, and panting grunts at that. Their movements were confusing, because they contradicted each other. They were struggling for survival, of course, but their method of attack was symbolic. The driver, after all, chose this speed to terrify them with.

"Indiscriminate accusations of this sort must not go unanswered. An investigation, therefore, is called for—not by some bland board—but by a special one created to look into the water-skiing menace.

"Who can forget that in the not too distant past, a hooded gang of murderers roamed Southern California mountains, preying upon fishing victims? Thanks to the sacrifice of brave sharpshooters on surrounding ridges, that evil is now subdued. But water-skiing?

"High speed boats! Frantic screaming! Water skis! What hideous nightmares must haunt tonight the grandmothers of Southern California!

"That brings to mind the recent evening when, exhausted and battered, I sought refuge from this conflict on the shore of Big Bear Lake. There, in an idyllic clearing separating me from the terror of the boat, a middle-aged woman sat beside the flames of her campfire, reading a book.

"'Lady,' I said, 'what is the meaning of this?'

"'Meaning?' she replied, closing the book. 'Why, this is Zen. I seek peace and tranquility.'

"Zen, I said, was not meant to replace courage, and water skiing is not necessarily a hobby for sissies. One can enjoy water skiing without risking serious injury to one's vital organs. Safety regulations, coupled with common sense, can keep the sport at a reasonable altitude.

"But what could we do? The answer didn't come easy. Then I saw the solution—an invisible canopy over Southern California lakes, effectively limiting boat speeds and reducing water skiing to a more soothing sport.

"Astounded voices cried out, 'Impossible!Fantastic! Marvelous!' I was prepared for such an outburst, but Mrs. Grandmother couldn't contain her shock. 'Impossible!' she exclaimed. 'And what about all the grandmothers? How will they enjoy their picnics?'

"Poor souls. Yes, the grandmothers. But there are ways to protect them, too—for instance, by banning boats entirely.

"But what then? you ask, aghast. Won't this deprive nature of its music, sanitarily rob mankind of an exhilarating sport? And won't the grandmothers be denied the pleasure of their picnics?

"Not at all. We shall have music, nature will not be robbed and, thanks to the grandmothers, mankind will be the richer.

"Nature will still have her music—only it will be more melodious. The staccato boom of excited motorboats will give way to a gentle lullaby, carried by a steady breeze.

"Water skiing will not be robbed, but rather ennobled. Without the roar of horsepower, the skier will hear faintly in the distance the gentle voice of nature singing him onward. Many skiers will doubtlessly find this change most welcome.

"And what of the grandmothers and all those who yearn for picnics free from the din of motorboats? They, too, will benefit immeasurably. With serene lakes and parks, devoid of boats and their accompanying noises, they will join hands and sing—dance and romp in childlike abandon—while nature ditties their gayety.

"But won't such a canopy, you ask, prove impractical to maintain?

"Not at all. Repairing tiny holes will be a simple matter. And remember, the cost will be more than overshadowed by the saving resulting from the permanent retirement of so many boats.

"So let us build this grand canopy to shield our peaceful haven from the ravenous and ruthless speed demons.

"But why stop there? Why not extend this principle to every phase of modern life? Let us build canopies over our cities, our states and our nation! A grand and protective umbrella to shield us all from the heedless, the hasty and the greedy.

"Thus undone, confusion, superficiality and hate will flee our golden shores, leaving behind only those whose hearts and minds are pure, attuned to the music of the spheres.

"Troubled generations will look upon our enlightened land and dwell among us—contented grandmothers, composers, poets, artists, dancers, singers and all manner of sensitive and refined beings.

"Our canopies may prove impractical, but isn't Utopia always a pip in the pipe dream department?"

